an  Russian Ukrainian polish Hungarian  Bulgarian Slovenian Serbian and Croatian  all exhibit five features languages with  fewer than five features are not really  considered part of standard average  European even though there may be some  per if  influence now let's take a look at those  nine features number one having both  definite and indefinite articles like  tha and ah in English only about one  third of the world's languages have  articles and the number of languages  with both definite and indefinite  articles is much smaller so it's  remarkable that in Europe so many  languages have both and that's evidence  of the standard average European schmuck  wound which languages have these  features all the romance languages all  Germanic languages except for Icelandic  which is the most geographically  peripheral Germanic language some Vulcan  languages including modern Greek and  maybe Albanian and Bulgarian which don't  have it Baltic and Slavic languages  possibly aside from Bulgarian here we  can see the German definite and  indefinite articles in all of their  inflected forms here we can see the  French definite and indefinite articles  and here we see the Hungarian definite  and indefinite articles number two  relative clauses with relative pronouns  these are relative clauses that come  after the noun with a relative pronoun  that changes depending on the function  of the head of the sentence for instance  in English the man who drives the car  versus the man whose car I drove in  German diamond a DeSoto Fiat  in German the relative pronoun comes  from a demonstrative pronoun but in most  cases the relative pronoun comes from an  interrogative pronoun in other words a  question word like in French  lunk economy negative and in modern  Greek the heat of the kimeta as you can  see from the map this feature is  widespread even outside of the core  standard average European languages  number three the have perfect like I  have finished the report in English t