nguage it doesn't appear in is  Romanian it's also absent from Slavic  Languages Uralic languages and others in  German neimand come and in French person  Avenue if we ignore no the main negation  word pas is not here to negate the verb  and these days people often skip the nut  anyway in Spanish and Italian this type  of negation is used but if the negative  pronoun is an object after the verb as  opposed to the subject then the verb is  negated for  example in Spanish no he visto another  seven comparisons of equality based on  an adverbial relative clause in English  we have comparisons like as tall as me  in German this would be Zulus V with me  being a relative adverb in other words  it's used to introduce a relative clause  like in this sentence he migui dude  angst meaning I like how you think in  English as is not used to introduce  relative clauses like that but it  derives from else wah or also which was  used as a relative pronoun in the past  and in modern Greek dos up si lo also  ago this feature appears in the romance  languages West Germanic languages Slavic  languages Hungarian Albanian and modern  Greek it's not found in North Germanic  languages finish the Celtic languages  and so on number eight non-pro drop  perhaps a more precise term for this is  strict subject agreement in most  languages verbs usually have a fixes  that indicate the subject and very  commonly the explicit subject pronoun  can be dropped for example in Arabic a  hit  which means I love you when spoken to a  female we know from the prefix aa that  this is first person singular so the  subject and the meaning I is often left  out but in certain European languages  including the course standard average  European languages of French and German  as well as Dutch English Welsh and  Icelandic the subject pronouns cannot be  dropped even though there are a fixes  that agree with the subject for example  in German ich liebe dich the subject  pronoun here is necessary and can't be  dropped this is one feature that only  applies to a limited number of standard  average European languages in Spanish  for example do TEM means I love you  Spanish is Pro drop the subject pronoun  here can be left out de amel but as a  side note in Spain it would be more  common to express love by saying you're  taquita  or dakedo meaning I want you but in  French it's shoot in French is non Pro  drop so the subject pronoun is required  even though syntactically it completely  lines up with the Spanish sentence if  the pronoun is included in the Spanish  sentence  number nine intensifier reflexive  distinction in English we used the word  self and its derivatives myself himself  and so on as an intensifier for example  the president himself gave a speech at  our school in the sentence the word  himself is an intensifier it emphasizes  that it's the President and not someone  else who gave a speech at the school but  in English and in many languages such  intensifiers are also used as reflexive  pronouns as well for example he loves  himself  but a typical feature of standard  average European languages is that  different words are used for the  intensifier and for the reflexive in  German the president zest meaning the  president himself and leap day meaning  he loves himself and in Polish some  president and cohosh shabbiha again we  have the intensifier and the reflexive  pronoun which are different in French  the prezi don't remember and is him this  is the direct translation of he loves  himself in French but this isn't really  a natural sentence for that meaning so  another example with a reflexive pronoun  is sooth would only mullah meaning he  sees himself in the mirror  obviously this differentiation does not  occur in English or in Dutch either but  it occurs in many standard average  European languages including those which  tend to have fewer Shiprock boned  features like the North Germanic  languages Latvian and Lithuanian and the  Slavic languages even Basque Basque is a  language isolate and not considered a  standard average European language  because it doesn't share enough of the  sprach bone features but it does share a  couple of them this is a good example of  how a sprach bone can have varying  degrees of impact on different languages  depending on geography and contact those  nine features are considered highly  likely to be part of a European Shiprock  boot there are numerous other features  which may be part of the sprat boot as  well but are less certain one such  feature is SVO word order  SVO for example in English I love  languages SVO and in German  SVO SVO is not limited to Europe but  within Europe it seems to correspond  well with the other core standard  average European features there's also  an absence of certain features that are  common in other regions of the world one  example is the absence of reduplicating  reduplicating is the repetition of a  word or part of a word usually to change  something about its meaning or function  for example in Indonesian there's the  word Ana which means child and anatomic  is the plural form meaning children  this type of reduplicating is almost  totally absent from standard average  European languages how did this Brock  bunte arise it's hard to know exactly  what historical events or conditions  contributed to the spread of standard  average European features but according  to Martin Haspel mass who's the source  of most of the information in this video  the most likely hypothesis is that it  happened during the migration period  between roughly 300 and 700 seee during  the transition from Late Antiquity to  the early Middle Ages during this time  the Western Roman Empire declined and  collapsed which triggered widespread  invasions into and within Europe  particularly into the territory of the  Empire the resulting migrations must  have brought many languages into contact  with each other of course this may be  responsible for some of the Schrock  Bunte features while other features may  have spread as a result of different  historical events one of the most  fascinating things about languages is  that they are a result of everything  that has happened to their speakers and  their ancestors up until this point the  question of the day is the rock Bunte in  your region or is there another sprach  book you've heard about what kind of  shared features are there let us know in  the comments down below if you enjoyed  this video then be sure to check out  Lange focus on the various social media  platforms like Facebook YouTube Twitter  and Instagram and as always I want to  say thank you to my patreon supporters  especially these wonderful patreon  supporters right here on the screen my  top tier patreon supporters many extra  special thanks to them and to everyone  thank you for watching and have a nice  day  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]

